  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 446 PM EDT MON SEP 28 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Wednesday morning with upper level \nridging in place over the Southeast CONUS, mirrored at the surface \nby high pressure offset to the east over the Western Atlantic. This \nwill be maintaining a mild southerly flow across the Ohio Valley. \nLooking upstream, a mean 500 hPa trough will be in place over \nwestern Canada while a closed ~568 dam low spins over the Lower \nColorado River Valley. The trough will be supporting an ~991 mb \nsurface low over the Upper Mackenzie River Valley with a  cold front \nextending southward into the Northern High Plains and then westward \nacross the Columbia River Basin. Another ~1000 mb low is noted over \nthe Southern Rockies ahead of the aforementioned closed low. \n\nFor the first few days of the long-term, the upper level ridging \nwill drift eastward to off the Southeast Coast. This will keep \nsoutherly flow and an unseasonably warm air mass (mean 850 hPa \ntemperatures ~16-18C, as per the LREF) and mostly dry conditions in \nplace over eastern Kentucky through Friday. In the meantime, the \nclosed low over the Desert Southwest will open up and be captured \nthe eastward-propagating northern-stream trough. As that trough \nshifts eastward, it will suppress the 500 hPa ridging over the Ohio \nValley, transitioning the flow aloft to quasi-zonal/weakly troughed. \nThe cold front associated with the trough will move into the Ohio \nValley but then struggle to make further southeastward progress \nFriday night-Saturday night as energy ejects out of the opening low \nand rides along the boundary. Meanwhile, PWATs could exceed the 90th \npercentile if the NAEFS/ECMWF mean solution materializes, amidst \ndeep moisture advection from the Gulf of America. If ingredients \ncome together ideally, a decent soaking rainfall is possible across \nportions of eastern Kentucky or adjacent regions this weekend, but \nforecaster confidence is low. From a more sensible standpoint, this \npattern will bring back the return of a summer-like feel for the \nremainder of the work week with dew points recovering back through \nthe 50s and into the 60s while afternoon temperatures peak in the \nlow to mid 80s from Wednesday through Friday. Some of the hotter \nportions of the Cumberland could flirt with the upper 80s. Rain \nchances will then knock back the high temperatures for Saturday but \nit will still be quite humid with the threat of rainfall and \npossibly a few rumbles of thunder.  \n\nChange will be around the corner to close out the first weekend of \nOctober. Within the broad upper level troughing, a quick-moving \nshortwave trough transiting the 49th Parallel (US-Canada border) \nover the weekend will phase with northern stream energy diving \nsoutheast from the Yukon. This will bring height falls to the Ohio \nValley early next week while slinging an associated cold front \nacross the Coalfields Sunday into Monday. Behind that front, LREF \nmean 850 hPa temperatures drop to ~9-11C with much drier PWATs (0.6 \nto 0.8 inches) by late Monday. Rain chances taper off behind the \nfirst cold front on Sunday and appear minimal with this second \nboundary. Instead, the more noticeable effect will be a decline in \ntemperatures to more seasonable levels for Monday with highs \nreturning to the mid 60s to lower 70s and cool nighttime lows in the \n40s and 50s.",
